plication","question":"With $x = t^2$, which integral equals $\\int_0^4 f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ using Type 2 substitution on $t\\in[0,2]$?","options":["$\\int_0^2 f(t^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$\\int_0^2 f(t^2)2t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$\\int_0^4 f(2t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$\\int_0^4 f(t)t^2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"19a7f5912239b77ef7a666bc"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_2_8698067c_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $x = \\ln(t-1)$, which expression represents $\\mathrm{d}x$?","options":["$\\frac{1}{t-1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$(t-1)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$\\ln(t-1)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1901782a711512a746c86c4b"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_2_8698067c_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement best captures how Type 2 is complementary to the $t = g(x)$ approach?","options":["Type 2 helps when no obvious derivative component is in the integrand","Type 2 is the same formula with the same starting substitution.","Type 2 is used only when the function is not Riemann integrable.","Type 2 avoids changing limits of integration."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c4fd98085b6a1cf2c112c24a"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_2_8698067c_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which missing condition would make the stated Type 2 theorem unsafe to apply?","options":["Not knowing whether $\\varphi$ is invertible.","Not knowing whether $\\varphi$ is the identity function.","Not knowing whether $\\varphi$ is continuously differentiable.","Not knowing whether $f$ is differentiable."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4460d28c90460e4514c74529"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_2_8698067c_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Using $x = \\ln(t-1)$ for $\\int_0^1 \\frac{1}{1+\\mathrm e^x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, what are the corresponding $t$-limits?","options":["$2$ and $1+\\mathrm e$","$0$ and $1$","$1$ and $\\mathrm e$","$-1$ and $0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"68db95b219affc901a41f6fb"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_2_8698067c_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $x = \\varphi(t) = t^3$ with $t\\in[1,2]$. Which expression is the Type 2 transform of $\\int_1^8 f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$?","options":["$\\int_1^2 f(t^3)t^3\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$\\int_1^8 f(t)3t^2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$\\int_1^2 f(t^3)3t^2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$\\int_1^8 f(t^3)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a2ef17ff33c5878cbcc6bdf1"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_2_8698067c_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What variable form starts Type 2 substitution?","options":["$t=g(x)$","$S_N=\\sum a_n$","$f'(x)=0$","$x=\\varphi(t)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6035592a580077df7cec58d3"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_2_8698067c_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Does $\\varphi$ need to be invertible in the stated Type 2 formula?","options":["No, invertibility is not required","Yes, it must always be bijective","Only when $f$ is constant","Only when the interval is open"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3bfdc50daf80db1fda63ea44"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_2_8698067c_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What happens to $\\mathrm{d}x$ when $x=\\varphi(t)$?","options":["It becomes $f'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","It becomes $\\mathrm{d}t/\\varphi(t)$","It disappears without replacement","It becomes $\\varphi'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2a7fc5577b